
TC35874 to UVC gadget application README
by Kin Wei Lee September 2020
Updated for Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm / Trixie


Original Work
=============
UVC original work by
Laurent Pinchart ideasonboard.com
and patches by Bhupesh Sharma
but conveniently collated by jdonald (github.com/jdonald/uvc-gadget)
V4L2 MMAL original work by
6by9 github.com/6by9/v4l2_mmal

Overview
=========
This software uses a Raspberry Pi Zero and a TC35874 bridge to convert an HDMI
source signal and output the video over USB so that it can be used as a USB
webcam.

The TC35874 outputs UYVY or RGB24 data to the CSI-2 camera port and is
incompatible with UVC gadget, which requires either YUYV or MJPEG formatted
data. The Pi's VideoCore hardware converts and scales the image (ISP) and,
for MJPEG, compresses it (JPEG encoder).

What changed for Bookworm / Trixie
===================================
Earlier versions used MMAL and the /opt/vc userland libraries (libmmal,
libvcos, libbcm_host, libvcsm). Raspberry Pi removed those after Buster, which
is why the program stopped working on Bullseye and later.

This version drives the same hardware through the kernel's V4L2 mem2mem
driver (bcm2835-codec) instead, and shares buffers between devices as
dmabufs from the CMA dma-heap:

  -f0 YUYV : unicam -> ISP (/dev/video12) -> UVC
  -f1 MJPEG: unicam -> ISP (/dev/video12) -> JPEG encoder (/dev/video31) -> UVC

No extra libraries are needed. It builds on 32-bit and 64-bit Pi OS.

Other changes:
 - Devices are auto-detected (UVC gadget, ISP, JPEG encoder); -u/-i/-j override.
 - Capture size defaults to the detected HDMI timings (-g still works).
 - -b (bitrate) is ignored. Use -q 1..100 (JPEG quality, default 80).
 - -r accepts 0/1/2 or WxH. It must match the size given to uvc-setup.sh.
 - Stopping and restarting a stream from the host now works without
   restarting the program. Ctrl-C exits cleanly.
 - uvc-setup.sh adds the descriptors that 6.x kernels require. It also takes
   an optional size, and 'stop' removes the gadget properly.
 - New hdmi-setup.sh does the EDID / DV-timings steps. On kernels where unicam
   is media-controller based, it also configures the media graph.

Prequisites
===========
Raspberry Pi Zero, Zero W or Zero 2 W (other boards with VideoCore IV should
also work; the Pi 5 does NOT, as it has no bcm2835-codec)
TC358743 based converter board
Raspberry Pi OS (Lite or Desktop) Bookworm or Trixie, 32- or 64-bit

  sudo apt install build-essential v4l-utils
  make

Setup
======
1. Edit /boot/firmware/config.txt (it is no longer /boot/config.txt):

     camera_auto_detect=0
     dtoverlay=tc358743,i2c_pins_28_29=1
     dtoverlay=dwc2,dr_mode=peripheral

   1080p UYVY buffers need about 40MB of CMA. If you get dma-heap allocation
   errors, raise it on the existing vc4-kms-v3d line, e.g.

     dtoverlay=vc4-kms-v3d,cma-128

   (Add i2c_pins_28_29=1 only if your board uses those pins, as the
   H2C-RPI0-B01 does on the Pi Zero.) raspi-config no longer needs 'Camera'
   enabled. Remove any old 'modules-load=dwc2,libcomposite' or 'cma=' from
   /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt; they are no longer needed. Reboot.

2. Make sure your user is in the 'video' group (default user already is).

3. Create the USB gadget (format and size must match step 5):
     sudo ./uvc-setup.sh start mjpeg 1280x720
   or
     sudo ./uvc-setup.sh start yuyv 1280x720

4. Set up the TC358743 (EDID + timings):
     ./hdmi-setup.sh                 # uses 1080P50EDID.txt
   This replaces the manual v4l2-ctl --set-edid / --set-dv-bt-timings steps.

5. Run
     ./v4l2-mmal-uvc -v /dev/video0 -f1 -r1      # MJPEG 1280x720
     ./v4l2-mmal-uvc -v /dev/video0 -f0 -r1      # YUYV  1280x720
   Add -d for debug output, -h for all options.

ConfigFS can be unloaded using
     sudo ./uvc-setup.sh stop

Troubleshooting
===============
"No UVC gadget video device found"
    Run uvc-setup.sh first. Check 'ls /sys/class/udc' lists a controller. If
    not, the dwc2 overlay is missing.
"ENOLINK" / "No HDMI signal"
    Run hdmi-setup.sh. Check the source is on and outputs a mode in the EDID.
"STREAMON failed with EPIPE"
    Media-controller kernel and the formats in the graph do not match. Run
    hdmi-setup.sh again with the same pixel format: UYVY8_1X16 for -zUYVY,
    RGB888_1X24 for -zRGB24/-zBGR24. 'media-ctl -p' shows the graph.
"dma-heap allocation ... failed"
    Increase CMA (see step 1).
Colours swapped with RGB input
    Use -zBGR24 instead of -zRGB24 (or the other way round).

Resolution
==========
The output resolution defaults to 1280x720 (-r1). Expect about 30fps with
MJPEG, or about 8.8fps with YUYV RAW (limited by USB isochronous bandwidth,
see below).

NOTES
======

Audio is NOT handled.

This software originally spliced the V4L2 mmal part of v4l2-mmal by 6by9 with
the UVC part of uvc-gadget by Laurent Pinchart. The UVC part is unchanged. The
MMAL part has been replaced by V4L2 mem2mem.

Capture, ISP and JPEG buffers are dmabufs shared between the devices (zero
copy). The only CPU copy is the finished frame into the UVC gadget's buffer
(V4L2_MEMORY_MMAP).

Internally the code will select UYVY output from TC35874 (RGB24 can be used
too with -zRGB24).

Interlaced sources: use -F interlaced (and an RGB24 source, -zRGB24), as
previously needed for the EOS M.

ISOCH USB transfers work.
BULK mode transfer does not work (f_uvc has no bulk support).

Framerate:
In YUYV mode the max framerate is about 8.8fps. It is limited by the
microframe period and the streaming_maxpacket size for the gadget.
microframe period = 125us (ISOCH transfer)
image size = 1280*720*2
streaming_maxpacket = 2048
framerate = 1/(1280*720*2/2048*125e-6) = 8.8fps
You can try MAXPACKET=3072 sudo -E ./uvc-setup.sh start yuyv (newer dwc2
drivers may cope).

ConfigFS is used to setup the UVC USB gadget.

The software may or may not work for you and it may kill your hardware. My first
PiZero's USB OTG functionality died during development. Essentially it no longer
works in gadget mode. The cause could have come from power coming from two
different sources into the Pi Zero.

Test Setup (original, Buster)
==============================
Hardware
-H2C-RPI0-B01 HDMI to CSI-2 Bridge using TC358743 (same foot print as Pi Zero)
-Pi Zero W (4.19.122+ recompiled from source using rpi-4.19.y branch)
-Pi Zero 2 W using stock Raspberry Pi OS Lite (Legacy)

Input HDMI sources:
-PC 1280x720 and 1920x1080 ok
-EOS M - 720x480 interlaced signal needs interlace mode (-F interlaced) and
the source must be RGB24. (use Magic Lantern for clean HDMI!)
-EOS M5 - 1920x1080 signal ok

Software on MacBook Pro (Retina, 13-inch, Late 2013)
-Facetime (Cataline 10.15.5) working (!)
-QuickTime Player working
-VLC (macOS 3.0.11.1) - selecting webcam gadget fails on first try and reverts
to the internal webcam, on second select webcam gadget -> facetime camera ->
webcam gadget to get it working. VLC however has a large latency.

Windows 10
- Skype working use UVC Camera as input and MJPEG mode, YUYV didn't work
- VLC doesn't work error 'Your input couldn't be opened'

The original MMAL sources are kept in legacy-mmal/ for reference.
